-- The interface between the Evidence Act 1893 and the Criminal Procedure Code 2010.
--
-- THIS FILE IS DECLARE-ONLY AND IS BYTE-IDENTICAL IN BOTH SUBJECTS:
--   sg/evidence-act-1893/encodings/ea-cpc-interface.l4
--   sg/criminal-procedure-code-2010/encodings/ea-cpc-interface.l4
-- It exists because `l4` resolves an IMPORT only to a module in the importing file's own
-- directory (or the project root, or the embedded library): an encoding in one subject cannot
-- import a module from another.  So the two Acts do not copy each other's rules; they meet at
-- the small set of conclusions each hands the other, declared here once and copied.
-- ../NOTES.md section 4 explains the arrangement and `check.sh` in each row checks the two
-- copies are identical.
--
-- Direction of the records:
--   `Evidence Act Conclusions`  are answers the EVIDENCE ACT row computes and the CPC row consumes.
--   `Code Conclusions`          are answers the CPC row computes and the EVIDENCE ACT row consumes.
-- Neither Act's rule is restated on the other side: a field here is a question, with the
-- provision that asks it, and the answer is supplied by the Act that owns the rule.

§ `Kinds of proceedings`

@ref "Evidence Act 1893, ss 2(1), 5, 23, 54, 55, 122, 134; Criminal Procedure Code 2010, s 268"
DECLARE `Kind of Proceedings` IS ONE OF
    `criminal proceedings`
    `civil proceedings`
    `other proceedings`

§ `Kinds of report and statement`

-- The CPC and the Evidence Act do not use "report" or "statement" in one sense.  Each of these
-- has its own treatment, and the fact that an offence was reported is not the content of the report.
@ref "Criminal Procedure Code 2010, ss 14, 15, 257 to 264A; Evidence Act 1893, ss 8(2), 17, 32, 147, 159"
DECLARE `Kind of Report or Statement` IS ONE OF
    `the fact that an offence was reported`
    `the content of a first information report`
    `a witness's statement made in the course of an investigation`
    `an accused's statement`
    `a Case for the Defence`
    `a conditioned statement`
    `a statement recorded as an audiovisual recording`
    `an affidavit sworn by a witness`
    `a report of a qualified person`
    `a document produced as evidence`
    `a deposition`

§ `What the Evidence Act tells the Code`

@ref "Criminal Procedure Code 2010, ss 259(1)(a), (b), (c), (e), 268, 269(1), 291(5)(a)"
DECLARE `Evidence Act Conclusions` HAS
    `the statement is admitted under section 147 of the Evidence Act 1893` IS A BOOLEAN
    `the statement is used for impeaching credit in the manner provided in section 157 of the Evidence Act 1893` IS A BOOLEAN
    `the statement is made admissible in criminal proceedings by another provision of the Evidence Act 1893` IS A BOOLEAN
    `the statement falls within section 32(1)(a) of the Evidence Act 1893` IS A BOOLEAN
    `the statement is admissible as evidence of a fact stated in it under the Evidence Act 1893` IS A BOOLEAN
    `the opinion of an expert is admissible under the Evidence Act 1893` IS A BOOLEAN
    `the accused is entitled by section 122(4) of the Evidence Act 1893 to refuse to answer the question` IS A BOOLEAN

§ `What the Code tells the Evidence Act`

@ref "Evidence Act 1893, ss 32(4)(a), 122(5)"
DECLARE `Code Conclusions` HAS
    `the notice requirements and other conditions prescribed under section 428 of the Criminal Procedure Code 2010 are complied with` IS A BOOLEAN
    `evidence of the fact is admissible by virtue of section 265 or 266 of the Criminal Procedure Code 2010` IS A BOOLEAN
